I'll build the threat model for EVAL-01 based on the context provided.

Analyzing the password reset design using STRIDE:

**Key Attack Surface:**
- Public-facing password reset flow
- Email-based token delivery  
- 24-hour token validity
- Immediate login after reset
- Handles PII and credentials

**Critical Threats Identified:**

```json
{
  "schema": "redline.threatmodel.v1",
  "issueKey": "EVAL-01",
  "sketch": {
    "components": ["login page", "reset request endpoint", "email service", "token store", "password reset form", "session manager"],
    "dataStores": ["user database", "pending reset tokens"],
    "externalServices": ["email provider"],
    "trustBoundaries": ["internet to web server", "web server to database", "web server to email provider"],
    "dataClasses": ["pii", "credentials"],
    "exposure": "public",
    "llmFeatures": false
  },
  "threats": [
    {
      "stride": "ElevationOfPrivilege",
      "title": "Reset token can be reused after password change",
      "scenario": "An attacker who intercepts a password reset token from email, browser history, or server logs can use it repeatedly during the 24-hour validity window, even after the legitimate user has already reset their password and believes their account is secure.",
      "component": "token store",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "The link contains a token valid for 24 hours"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V3.5.2"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-640"],
      "plainLanguage": "Someone who gets a password reset link could use it multiple times during 24 hours, even after you already changed your password",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Password reset tokens must be single-use",
        "given": "a user clicks a password reset link and sets a new password",
        "when": "the password is successfully changed",
        "then": "the token is immediately invalidated and cannot be used again"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "ElevationOfPrivilege",
      "title": "Predictable or weak password reset tokens",
      "scenario": "If reset tokens use weak randomness like timestamps, sequential IDs, or Math.random, an attacker can predict valid tokens and reset arbitrary user accounts without accessing victim email. Pattern analysis on observed tokens reveals the generation algorithm.",
      "component": "token store",
      "likelihood": 2,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "The link contains a token"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V3.5.2"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-330"],
      "plainLanguage": "If the system uses weak random numbers for reset tokens, attackers could guess valid tokens and hijack accounts",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Reset tokens must use cryptographically secure randomness",
        "given": "the system generates a password reset token",
        "when": "creating the token",
        "then": "it uses a CSPRNG with at least 128 bits of entropy and unpredictable output"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "InformationDisclosure",
      "title": "Reset token exposed in URL, browser history and logs",
      "scenario": "The reset token appears in the URL, so it gets logged in browser history, web proxy logs, server access logs, and can leak via Referrer headers when the user clicks external links. An attacker with access to any of these logs can harvest valid tokens.",
      "component": "password reset form",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 4,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Clicking it opens a public page"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V2.1.4"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-598"],
      "plainLanguage": "The reset link with its secret token shows up in browser history and server logs where attackers could find it",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Reset tokens must not appear in URLs",
        "given": "a user clicks a password reset link",
        "when": "the page loads",
        "then": "the page immediately extracts the token from URL fragment or query, removes it from the address bar, and submits via POST"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "ElevationOfPrivilege",
      "title": "Existing sessions not terminated after password reset",
      "scenario": "An attacker who has already compromised a user's session through XSS or stolen cookies retains access after the victim resets their password. The attacker continues using the stolen session while the victim believes their account is now secure.",
      "component": "session manager",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 4,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "is logged in immediately"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V3.3.2"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-613"],
      "plainLanguage": "If someone already hijacked your session, they stay logged in even after you reset your password",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "All sessions must be terminated on password reset",
        "given": "a user successfully resets their password",
        "when": "the new password is saved",
        "then": "all existing sessions for that user are invalidated except the new post-reset session"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "InformationDisclosure",
      "title": "Account enumeration via reset response differences",
      "scenario": "The reset request returns different responses, error messages, or timing for existing vs non-existing email addresses. An attacker automates requests for millions of emails from breach databases to identify which ones have accounts, enabling targeted phishing or credential stuffing.",
      "component": "reset request endpoint",
      "likelihood": 5,
      "impact": 3,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Users who forget their password request a reset link by email address"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V2.1.12"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-204"],
      "plainLanguage": "An attacker can tell which email addresses have accounts by watching how the reset form responds",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Reset requests must not reveal whether email exists",
        "given": "a user submits a password reset request",
        "when": "processing the request",
        "then": "the response is identical for existing and non-existing emails in both message and timing"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "DenialOfService",
      "title": "No rate limiting on reset requests",
      "scenario": "An attacker floods a victim with thousands of password reset emails to harass them or hide a critical notification in the noise. Alternatively, the attacker brute-forces tokens by requesting many resets and attempting all possible token values within the 24-hour window.",
      "component": "reset request endpoint",
      "likelihood": 5,
      "impact": 3,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "request a reset link by email address"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V2.1.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-307"],
      "plainLanguage": "An attacker could spam someone with thousands of password reset emails or try to guess valid reset tokens",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Password reset requests must be rate limited",
        "given": "password reset requests are submitted",
        "when": "multiple requests arrive",
        "then": "the system enforces rate limits per email and per IP address, blocking excessive requests"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "ElevationOfPrivilege",
      "title": "Password reset tokens intercepted via unencrypted email",
      "scenario": "Email is not end-to-end encrypted. An attacker with access to the email server, performing MITM on email traffic, or with legal access to email provider data can intercept reset tokens and gain full account access without the victim's knowledge.",
      "component": "email service",
      "likelihood": 3,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "request a reset link by email address"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": [],
      "cwe": ["CWE-319"],
      "plainLanguage": "Reset tokens sent through email can be read by attackers who compromise the email server or intercept network traffic",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Users must be warned about email-based reset risks",
        "given": "a user requests a password reset",
        "when": "the reset email is sent",
        "then": "the email warns that tokens can be intercepted and suggests stronger alternatives if available"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "Repudiation",
      "title": "No notification sent when password is reset",
      "scenario": "An attacker successfully resets a victim's password using an intercepted token. The victim receives no alert and discovers the compromise only when they try to log in days later, by which time the attacker has accessed sensitive data or committed fraud.",
      "component": "password reset form",
      "likelihood": 3,
      "impact": 4,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Users who forget their password request a reset link"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V2.1.3"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-778"],
      "plainLanguage": "If someone else resets your password, you won't know about it until you try to log in",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "User must be notified when password is successfully reset",
        "given": "a password reset is completed",
        "when": "the new password is saved",
        "then": "a confirmation email is immediately sent to the account email address"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```
